dget < sum_mins or budget > sum(s['capacity'] ...): raise? But better to return something? I'll just implement allocation as usual but will respect constraints: if budget too low, can't allocate mins, but we allocate mins first, then remaining. If budget < sum_mins, we cannot satisfy mins; maybe allocate mins but then total would be sum_mins not budget. The problem says \"exact total budget\". So we should strictly adhere to budget. But if budget < sum_mins, impossible. I'll assume valid inputs.\n- Initialize allocations list: [s['minimum'] for each segment].\n- remaining = budget - sum_mins.\n- While remaining > 0:\n  - For each segment i, compute score = s['importance'] / (allocations[i] + 1) # next position\n  - Find max score; ties broken by original order (i.e., first segment with max score).\n  - Increment allocations[i] by 1, remaining by -1.\n  - But must also check capacity: if allocations[i] == capacity, skip? Actually the algorithm says \"give one token to the segment maximizing importance divided by its next allocated position.\" But if segment already at capacity, its next allocated position would be capacity+1, but we cannot allocate. So we should not consider segments at capacity. So when computing score, only consider segments where allocations[i] < capacity. If none, then remaining budget cannot be allocated, but problem says budget <= total capacity? It says \"above total capacity\" reject. So if budget <= sum capacities, we should be able to allocate all. But if due to integer allocation, maybe some capacity left but not enough to allocate? Actually we allocate one at a time, so if budget <= sum caps, we can always allocate all. Because we only allocate to segments below capacity.\n- After loop, total = budget (or sum allocations which equals budget).\n- by_name: dict mapping segment name to its allocation. Since segments have unique names? Assume yes, but if duplicate? Not specified. We'll use the name from case.\n- Return dict with keys: allocations (list of ints in same order as segments), by_name (dict with name->allocation), total (int, =budget).\n\nEdge: tie-breaking by original segment order. The algorithm: \"Break equal priorities by original segment order.\" So if two segments have same score, the one earlier in the list gets token.\n\nImplementation in Python: use a loop.\n\nNow output format: Must be a JSON string containing the implementation. Enclose in {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. Escape properly.\n\nWrite the code. Use standard library only.\n\nAlso note: The schema for by_name says dynamicKeysFrom: \"case.segments[*].name\". So we must use exact names from input.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1384,"completion_tokens":1497,"total_tokens":2881,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1071},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":104},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}